# WHAT APIFY IS ALLOWED TO HOLD.
#
# `.dockerignore` controls what goes into the IMAGE. It does NOT control what
# `apify push` uploads: the CLI's own help says "Files matched by .gitignore and
# .actorignore are excluded" — .dockerignore is not in that list. So for the
# first deploy, on 2026-08-27, all 176 tracked files went to Apify's servers
# including docs/wire/ and probes/results/, while the image itself correctly
# held 26. The guard was real; it was just pointed at the wrong layer.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE SAME RULE, APPLIED WHERE THE UPLOAD HAPPENS. Keep the two in
# step: anything added to .dockerignore belongs here too.

# Dev material. `src/` imports none of it and the actor's CMD is
# `python -m src.main`, so none of it is reachable at runtime. Two reasons to
# withhold it, and the second is the important one:
#   1. an artefact should contain what it runs and nothing else
#   2. docs/wire/ and probes/results/ ARE the reverse-engineering — the measured
#      map of an API with no contract. That is the product. It does not belong
#      on a third party's servers as a side effect of deploying.
docs/
probes/
tests/
.claude/
api_sim.py
.github/
tools/

# Operator tooling for the machines that FILL the vault. Describes the
# operator's own infrastructure — proxies, profile ids, systemd units — and
# `src/` never imports it.
adspower/
browsers/

# Scripts and prose. A PATTERN, not a list of names: .dockerignore named
# run_local.sh and ship.sh individually and silently shipped preflight.sh and
# smoke.sh the day they were added.
*.sh
*.md

# ...except the prose that IS the product page. On the Apify Store the README is
# what a customer reads before deciding to run anything, and `*.md` above would
# have stripped it silently -- the actor would have shipped with a blank listing
# and nothing would have failed. Root-anchored `!/` is not enough here: this
# file lives in .actor/, so it needs its own path.
!.actor/README.md

# ...and the same shape one directory deeper, for the three persona actors.
#
# HONEST STATUS, because the first version of this comment overclaimed and was
# corrected the same day (2026-09-10). Each persona's actor.json sets
# `"readme": "./README.md"` -> actors/<persona>/README.md, and those paths match
# `*.md` above with nothing un-ignoring them. That looked like three blank Store
# listings. IT IS NOT: checked against the live platform, all three personas AND
# the general actor serve their full README today. The CLI uploads whatever file
# the manifest's `readme` field names, whether or not the ignore rules cover it.
#
# So this rule is belt-and-braces, not a repair. It is kept because relying on
# the CLI to override an explicit exclusion is relying on undocumented behaviour
# that a CLI upgrade could change silently -- and the failure mode, if it ever
# did change, is a listing with an empty body that fails nothing and is seen
# first by a paying customer. tests/test_ignore_parity.py checks all four.
!actors/*/README.md

# Never, under any circumstance.
.env
.env.*
# Root-anchored, and that leading slash is the whole point. Written as
# `!.env.example` it un-ignores that FILENAME ANYWHERE, which re-included
# .claude/worktrees/<branch>/.env.example and defeated the `.claude/` rule
# three lines up. Caught by pulling the deployed source back down and counting.
!/.env.example
